# Snapmark: how the system shows up

Snapmark is a Mac screenshot and feedback tool for people who work with AI agents. This page holds what is true of Snapmark and not of every product: the patterns, components, keys, colour extras and migration notes that moved out of the system's rules. Where the system says "In Snapmark", the detail is here. The decisions that taught us something are in [decisions/](decisions).

Nothing here binds another product. A pattern moves into [../patterns.md](../docs/patterns/patterns.md) only when a second product needs it.

## Colour extras (registered under `x`)

- `record`: recording in progress, the recording dot. Not the remove colour.
- Two marks keep a fixed look whatever colour the user picks, because the reader needs them: remove and highlight. The user's other marks use the colour they pick; the app's buttons use the app's own roles.
- Snapmark's names for the system roles: `--bg` (ground), `--panel` (panel), `--field` (raise), `--line` (edge), `--hover` (a soft fill on hover). Floating panels use the system's popover and HUD materials.

## Keys

- **Global capture family.** macOS uses Shift-Command with 3, 4 and 5 for screenshots, and keeps them for itself. Snapmark uses Control-Shift with the same digits: 3 captures every display, 4 captures the last area again, 5 opens the capture bar. N starts a new session. Taking the platform's own chords works only after the user turns the system's keys off; it was rejected.
- **The editor uses Preview's keys.** Arrows nudge a selected mark by 1 point (Shift for 10), Command-A selects all marks, Command-S saves.
- **The session window uses Finder's keys.** Return renames, Command-Delete removes, Command-O opens.
- **Single-key tool shortcuts.** C and 1 to 7 pick tools. They are switchable off and never fire in text. Command-1 to 7 always pick a tool.
- **Never take the recorded app's keys.** [decisions/08](decisions/08-do-not-take-the-recorded-apps-keys.md).

## Patterns

### Capture bar

**Level:** Product's choice.

**Job.** Choose what to capture and where it goes, then capture, without leaving the app in front.
**Source.** Modelled on the platform's own screenshot bar.

**Anatomy.** A non-activating panel at the bottom centre of the display with the pointer, over every Space and full-screen app.
- Top row: headline (the chosen mode), hint (what comes after Capture), the destination control and the session pop-up.
- Hairline.
- Bottom row: an Esc button, the modes numbered 1 to 6 (each with icon, name and key), Options, and one primary Capture button with its Return key.

**States.** Default. Lost focus (only Esc still works, globally). Empty (no area yet, no session yet): the hint says what comes next. Mode chosen: the chosen mode has a grey fill and an accent icon and label. Options open: a native menu. Disabled with reason: an option a mode cannot use is greyed, never hidden. Busy: one key means stop.

**Keys.** 1 to 6 pick a mode. Left and Right move. Return or Command-Return capture. Esc closes. Option-O opens Options.

**Rules.** Clicking a mode only chooses it. Capture alone takes the shot. The button names the outcome ("Capture One-Shot"). Options apply only to captures started from the bar, so a forgotten option never changes the everyday keys. The bar closes and waits 200 ms before capturing. The digits match the global keys where they share a meaning.

**Not for:** a product with one capture mode, or where a single chord is enough.

### Editor window

**Level:** Product's choice.

**Job.** Mark up one thing and commit it to the right place.

**Anatomy.** Toolbar on top in key order, the content in the middle, the notes column on the right (320 px). Discard and Add to Session sit bottom right, with Add as the one primary. The toolbar ends with the destination controls.

**States.** Default. Edit Again (the destination rests with a reason). One-Shot (a different primary: Copy Image, and the window closes after copying). Save failed (the window, the marks and the notes stay; the button comes back; a message says what to do). Recording (see key frames below).

**Keys.** Single-key tool shortcuts. Command-1 to 7 always pick a tool. Esc steps back one level. Command-Return commits. Command-W discards to a recoverable place.

**Not for:** a document with many pages.

### Toolbar of grouped tool keys (the instance of the system pattern)

One icon per key, all visible, the key as a caption under each icon. A key with several tools shows the current one with a dot or a stack. Pressing the key again swaps to the next tool. A colour swatch, Undo and Redo follow. A ghost of the next shape shows under the pointer after a tool key. Drawing tools always draw, even on top of marks. Only Select moves, resizes or deletes. Seven keys are quicker to choose from than twelve (Hick's Law). The owner asked for icons rather than word boxes and for the current tool to be the icon shown. The tooltip reads "Box (2 or Command-2) - press again for Ellipse".

### Notes column

**Level:** Product's choice.

**Job.** Say in words what the marks mean.

**Anatomy.**
- A "General Comment" heading over a box that covers the whole screenshot. The placeholder asks: "About the whole screenshot...".
- A hairline, then the "References" heading, shown only once a reference exists.
- One note per reference, each starting with its number in a diamond, with 12 pt between notes. Each note's placeholder asks about its number: "What about 1?".
- A footer, always in view, with the commit buttons.

**States.** Empty (only the general comment). With references. One-Shot (the column hides and a line says "Notes stay with Session. They are not copied."). Long notes grow to fit and the column scrolls.

**Keys.** Placing a reference puts the cursor in its note at once. Markdown renders as you type.

**Not for:** a form with required fields.

### Destination control (the instance of the system pattern)

A segmented control ("Session | One-Shot"), then a pop-up showing the current session and its count. The pop-up lists recent sessions with a check on the current one, then "Older Sessions...", then "New Session". In One-Shot the pop-up is greyed and reads "Not in a Session". Shift-Command-O switches the segment. Shift-Command-P opens the pop-up. See [decisions/05](decisions/05-destination-at-the-commit.md).

### Session window (a reader and a recovery place)

**Level:** Product's choice.

**Job.** Read what you collected, fix it, go back to what you discarded.

**Anatomy.** Tabs: Document (the file as the reader sees it), Screenshots (one image at a time with Previous, Next, a counter "2 of 3", Edit Again, Remove from Session), Discarded (kept 7 days, with Restore). The title is the session name; click to rename in place. The window opens at 90 percent of the work area and remembers its size.

**Keys.** Left and Right flip. Return renames. Command-O opens Edit Again. Command-Delete removes. Space plays a recording.

**Not for:** a product with nothing to recover.

### The menu bar menu

**Level:** Product's choice (menu labels name their object is Fixed).

**Job.** Be home for an app that has no main window.

**Anatomy.** A header that names the current object ("Adding Screenshots to <name>") or "No Session Yet". Capture rows with their keys. The current session's actions (Open Current Session, Copy Prompt for AI). A Session submenu (New, Switch, Rename, Export, Show in Finder, Copy path). Discarded Screenshots (only when there is something). Settings and Quit. Every item has an icon and names its object.

**Rules.** No explanation lines. Short. Rebuilt each time it opens. A key is shown only when the system accepted it. The same capture rows appear here as on the keyboard.

**Not for:** an app with a full menu bar of its own (put the commands there).

### Reviewing a recording by key frames

**Level:** Product's choice.

**Job.** Let a reader see what changed in a recording, without every frame.

**Anatomy.** The canvas is a grid of key frames, each labelled with its time. A facts line: "Recording - 3.2 s - 10 fps - 4 key frames of 32". A link to show all frames. The user marks frames like any screenshot. A marked frame is always a key frame. The session keeps a filmstrip PNG for the AI and a GIF at the real timing for people.

**Rules.** Frames that do not change are dropped, by hash and then by a pixel threshold. The first and the settled last frame always stay. At most 24 are chosen automatically. The GIF merges identical frames and sums their delays so playback keeps the real speed.

**Not for:** a recording the reader needs to scrub.

### Settings window (the instance of the system pattern)

One window with tabs (General, Capture, Shortcuts). Every change applies at once. A status line per setting that can be blocked, with a button that fixes it ("Open Accessibility Settings...").

## HUD panels

**Level:** Product's choice, for tools that watch another app. Fixed: no plain keys taken from the app being watched.

- **Countdown overlay:** the area outlined, a large digit in a frosted square at its centre, "Esc cancels". It lets clicks pass through. With Reduce Motion, digits change without scale or fade.
- **Recording and scrolling control:** one dark panel, 16 pt below the area (else above, else at the display edge, else inside it). Cancel as an X, a status ("0:07 of 0:30 - 10 fps"), a primary Done with its key, a hint ("Control-Shift-5 stops"). It never takes focus.
- **Rules:** content-protected so it never appears in what is captured. No plain key is taken from the app being recorded. The menu bar icon also stops or cancels.
- **Not for:** a tool that does not capture another app.

The capture bar, a panel that never activates the app, first drew its own tooltip label above the icon, then retired it once every control showed its name and key on screen.

## First run (proposed, not yet accepted)

One notice on the first launch ("Snapmark is in your menu bar..."), and a clear permission guide that never opens an editor on a blank wallpaper.

## Migration notes to the system's scale

- Interface text was 13 and key captions 11; both move to the type scale.
- The 36 px toolbar buttons move to 32 or 40. The capture bar's 40 and 48 fit. The 64 px mode buttons are a larger HUD size, to be decided (open).
- Components are built per window and move to a shared set ([../architecture.md](../docs/engineering/architecture.md)).
- The focus ring offset is 1 here, 2 in Samplewerk (open).

## Recorded exceptions

Deliberate departures from Snapmark's own rules, accepted 2026-10-26. The ones that bend a rule every product shares are written beside that rule in the system; the ones below are Snapmark's alone.

- **Redactions cannot be moved, resized or pasted.** Moving one would uncover what it hides. (Bends "select works like Preview".)
- **References have a fixed size.** An even size keeps every number readable across a session.
- **The area key uses the system's own capture overlay, not Snapmark's area picker.** It is the system's, and accessible as it is. The picker's slip rule (a click or a sliver starts over, Esc cancels) applies to the picker only.
- **Pen strokes are pointer only.** A freehand path cannot be typed: the one written exception to keyboard-complete ([../interaction.md](../docs/interaction/keyboard-complete.md#3-keyboard-complete) section 3).
- **Remove and Highlight ignore the picked colour.** Red and yellow carry a meaning the agent reads.
- **Single-key tools** on a canvas, switchable off, never in text ([../interaction.md](../docs/interaction/keyboard-complete.md#3-keyboard-complete) section 3).
- **Esc on Select closes the editor.** The screenshot goes to Discarded, so the close can be undone ([../interaction.md](../docs/interaction/keyboard-complete.md#3-keyboard-complete) section 3).
- **Quit with unsaved marks and Move to Trash ask first.** After them there is nothing left to undo ([../interaction.md](../docs/interaction/saving-and-undo.md#5-saving-undo-and-never-losing-work) section 5).
- **Copy keeps its notification.** The clipboard cannot show that it changed ([../interaction.md](../docs/interaction/feedback.md#6-feedback-in-proportion) section 6).
- **Global keys use Control-Shift, not Shift-Command.** The system keeps Shift-Command-3, 4 and 5 for itself.
- **The capture bar draws its own tooltips.** A non-activating panel never shows native tooltips; the label stays while the pointer rests on it and Esc dismisses it with the bar.

